Pod Protocol definations.
These are currently defined in Typescript (I am proficient in Typescript)
In the future these will be represented in ProtoBufs or QuickType (JSON)

These describe the request and acknoledgement events sent from client and sent by the Pod backend.
Supported by both Pods (the router) and Pod (the database) and all clients.

Read ./defination.ts for the message shapes. The semantics below are what
the Pod server implements.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Connection & namespaces (scopes)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ws://<host>:<port>/webchannel?ns=<namespace>&t=<token>

The namespace is fixed for the lifetime of the connection and every request
on it is confined to that namespace: the same path in two namespaces is two
different documents, and subscriptions only see commits in their namespace.
Without ?ns the server's DEFAULT_NAMESPACE (default "default") is used.
Namespaces are 1-64 characters of [A-Za-z0-9_-].

`t` is an optional token (see Authentication). Refused connections are
accepted and then immediately closed with a code clients must NOT retry
automatically (browsers cannot observe HTTP upgrade failures):
  4400  invalid namespace
  4401  token missing (POD_REQUIRE_AUTH), malformed, forged or expired, or
        a token sent to a server without POD_AUTH_SECRET
  4403  the token's "ns" claim does not include the namespace
Other close codes are transient and clients should reconnect with backoff:
  1013  try again later: backpressure limit exceeded (64 MiB queued), or,
        via a router, the namespace's database node is unavailable

Text frames carry JSON, binary frames carry MessagePack. Responses and events
use the encoding of the request that caused them. Requests on one connection
are processed strictly in order; responses carry the request's request_id.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Paths
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
'/'-separated, alternating collection/document: users (collection),
users/u1 (document), users/u1/posts (sub-collection). Leading/trailing '/'
are ignored. Rejected: empty segments (a//b), "." / "..", NUL bytes, invalid
UTF-8, segments of the form __name__, >100 segments, segments >1500 bytes.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Operations
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
READ        document path. Missing document: ACK status 404, data null.
WRITE       document path. merge=false replaces, merge=true deep-merges
            (objects merge recursively; arrays/null/scalars replace).
            Status 201 when created, 200 when updated. _v starts at 1 and
            increments on every change.
DELETE      document path: deletes the document AND every sub-collection
            beneath it. Collection path: deletes every document in it and
            beneath it. Atomic. affected_count = documents deleted.
QUERY       collection path; direct documents only (not sub-collections).
MUTATE_MANY collection path; applies `remove` (field paths) then `set`
            (field path -> value) to every document matching `where`,
            atomically. remove/set paths may not overlap. affected_count
            counts documents that actually changed.

Field paths use dot notation (user.emails). `project` returns only the listed
fields (all fields when empty/absent).

where filters are AND-ed; a missing field never matches:
  EQUAL                 deep equality (1 == 1.0)
  GREATER / LESSER / GREATER_EQUAL / LESSER_EQUAL
                        only match values of the same type class
  ARRAY.IN              field is an array containing `value`
  ARRAY.NOT_IN          field is an array not containing `value`
  ARRAY.EQUALS          field is an array equal to `value` (an array)
  ARRAY.ELEMENT_AT      value = {"index": n, "value": v}: field[n] == v
                        (negative n counts from the end)

Value order: null < boolean < number < string < array < object.

orderBy: documents missing an orderBy field are excluded. Ties are broken by
document id in the direction of the last orderBy clause (ascending if none).

limit: positive integer. next_cursor value is last document's ID that was in
the result set after limits. cursor property when applied causes backend to
retrieve document list, and then discards the document at cursor index and
everything before. If the cursor document changed or stopped matching since,
the page resumes after its current sort position; if it was deleted the
request fails with INVALID_CURSOR.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Subscriptions
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUBSCRIBE  document path, or collection path with optional where / orderBy /
           limit / project (where/orderBy/limit are rejected on documents).
           subscription_id is chosen by the client, unique per connection.
           Server sends: ACK(SUBSCRIBED), [EVENT RESET], EVENT SNAPSHOT.
Then for every commit affecting the result: EVENT ADDED / MODIFIED / REMOVED.
Applying deltas in order to the client array reproduces the server's view:
  REMOVED   remove at old_index
  ADDED     insert at new_index
  MODIFIED  remove at old_index, then insert the new document at new_index
Every event carries resume_token (the commit sequence number, a decimal
string) and commit_complete (true on the last event a commit produced for
the subscription). A client's view is consistent with resume_token only after
an event with commit_complete = true; that is the token to persist.
SUBSCRIBE with a resume_token still covered by the server's commit log
(POD_LOG_RETENTION commits, persisted with the data) answers resumed = true
and sends only the deltas between the view at that token and now, then
SYNCED. Otherwise (unknown or pruned token) it sends RESET then SNAPSHOT.
Clients ACK events with { meta: {type: "ACK", subscription_id}, payload:
{resume_token} }; this has no reply. UNSUBSCRIBE replies UNSUBSCRIBED
(NOT_FOUND if unknown). Closing the socket drops all its subscriptions. A client too slow to drain
its socket (64 MiB buffered) is disconnected rather than silently losing
events, so it can resubscribe.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Secondary indexes
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE_INDEX { collection, fields: [{field, mode?}] } indexes every
collection with that id (the last path segment, e.g. "posts" covers
users/*/posts) in the namespace, backfilling existing documents. The id is
deterministic ("posts:author,score"); 201 when created, 200 if it existed.
Mode VALUE (default) indexes the value; ARRAY_CONTAINS indexes each element
of an array (serves ARRAY.IN), at most one per index. An index is eligible
for a QUERY only if every indexed field appears in its where or orderBy
(documents missing an indexed field are not in the index). The planner binds
leading index fields with EQUAL (ARRAY.IN for ARRAY_CONTAINS) filters, uses
the next field for range bounds and ordering, and checks every remaining
filter on the fetched documents, so results are identical with or without
an index; QUERY's plan says which index (if any) was used. DROP_INDEX { id },
LIST_INDEXES {}. With security rules configured, index management requires
an admin token ("admin": true claim).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Authentication
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tokens are HS256 JWTs signed with the server's POD_AUTH_SECRET:
  sub    required, the user id (auth.uid in rules)
  exp    optional expiry (seconds); checked at connect (30 s leeway) and on
         every request (no leeway): requests on a connection whose token has
         expired fail with 401 UNAUTHENTICATED; reconnect with a new token
  ns     optional string or array: namespaces the token may connect to
  admin  optional, true allows index management
  ...    any other claims are visible to rules as auth.token.<claim>
Connections without a token are anonymous (auth == null) unless
POD_REQUIRE_AUTH is set.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Security rules
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
POD_RULES_PATH points at a JSON file; without it the server is open (any
client may do anything in its namespace). Format and semantics are
documented in src/rules/rules.h. In short:
  { "rules": [ { "match": "users/{uid}",
                 "allow": { "read": "auth != null && auth.uid == uid" } } ] }
Operations: get, list (read = both), create, update, delete (write = all
three). An operation is allowed if any matching rule's CEL condition is
true; no matching rule means denied. Enforcement:
  READ           get on the document (also when it does not exist)
  QUERY          documents failing list are silently left out
  SUBSCRIBE      document: get up front; collection: documents failing list
                 never appear in the snapshot or deltas
  WRITE / DELETE / MUTATE_MANY
                 every changed document is checked as create, update or
                 delete; one denial aborts the whole commit (atomic)
Denials are 403 PERMISSION_DENIED.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Cluster (pods router)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A router (POD_ROLE=router) speaks exactly this protocol to clients. It hashes
each connection's namespace onto a consistent-hash ring of the nodes in
POD_NODES and proxies the connection to the owning node over one
authenticated TCP link per node (POD_INTERNAL_PORT on the node; mutual
HMAC challenge-response over POD_CLUSTER_SECRET; framing in
src/cluster/frame.h). A namespace lives on exactly one node, so queries,
subscriptions, resume tokens and indexes behave as on a single node. Nodes
re-authenticate every proxied connection with their own POD_AUTH_SECRET.
If a node's link drops, its clients are closed with 1013 and should
reconnect (resuming subscriptions); the router relinks with backoff.
Membership is static: changing POD_NODES moves some namespaces to nodes that
do not hold their data (no migration yet).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Errors
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{ meta: { type: "ERROR", request_id, timestamp, status }, payload: { code, message } }
  400 MALFORMED_REQUEST  frame could not be decoded
  400 UNKNOWN_TYPE       unsupported meta.type
  400 INVALID_ARGUMENT   bad payload field
  400 INVALID_PATH       malformed path or wrong path kind
  400 INVALID_CURSOR     cursor document missing / invalid
  401 UNAUTHENTICATED    the connection's token has expired
  403 PERMISSION_DENIED  denied by security rules / not an admin
  404 NOT_FOUND          unknown subscription or index
  409 ALREADY_EXISTS     duplicate subscription_id
  413 DOCUMENT_TOO_LARGE encoded document over 4 MiB
  500 INTERNAL / DATA_CORRUPTION
